WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.537
Thread 1 fills in a payment.

2
00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:07.888
It writes the amount, then sets a flag called ready.

3
00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:10.626
Thread 2 waits until ready is true.

4
00:00:10.626 --> 00:00:12.454
Then it reads the amount.

5
00:00:12.454 --> 00:00:14.209
Is it sure to see 12000?

6
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.816
With plain fields, no.

7
00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:17.746
Nothing links the two threads.

8
00:00:17.746 --> 00:00:20.835
Thread 2 may read the new amount, or an old one.

9
00:00:20.835 --> 00:00:23.280
It may even never see ready turn true.

10
00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:27.281
The memory model answers with a relation called happens-before.

11
00:00:27.281 --> 00:00:31.096
If a write happens-before a read, the read must see that write.

12
00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:35.052
Inside one thread, each action happens-before the next one.

13
00:00:35.052 --> 00:00:36.912
That is called program order.

14
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:38.894
Now make ready volatile.

15
00:00:38.894 --> 00:00:40.493
Nothing else changes.

16
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:48.353
The volatile rule: a write to a volatile field happens-before every later read of that same field that sees the write.

17
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.450
The write is called a release.

18
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.197
The read that sees it is called an acquire.

19
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:54.568
Now chain the arrows.

20
00:00:54.568 --> 00:00:57.568
Writing the amount comes before the volatile write.

21
00:00:57.568 --> 00:00:59.567
The volatile read sees that write.

22
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:02.213
And the read comes before reading the amount.

23
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:05.722
So writing the amount happens-before reading it.

24
00:01:05.722 --> 00:01:09.520
Thread 2 must see 12000, though amount is a plain field.

25
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:13.640
One volatile field carries every write that came before it.

26
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.805
A write made after ready = true is not covered.

27
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.870
The rule says the same field.

28
00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:24.672
Say thread 2 reads a different volatile field, called checksum, and then reads the amount.

29
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:30.153
Both fields are volatile, but the write was to ready, and the read is of checksum.

30
00:01:30.153 --> 00:01:31.056
No edge forms.

31
00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:33.505
Thread 2 may read an old amount again.

32
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.613
A lock gives the same kind of edge.

33
00:01:36.613 --> 00:01:39.585
Leaving a synchronized block releases the lock.

34
00:01:39.585 --> 00:01:43.316
Entering a synchronized block on the same lock acquires it.

35
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.909
Here the two threads use different locks.

36
00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:49.901
Each block still runs one thread at a time, but no edge links them.

37
00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:56.097
So for every field two threads share, name the edge: the same volatile field, or the same lock.

38
00:01:56.097 --> 00:01:58.891
If you can't name one, you have a data race.
